<section class="mp-market-explainer" aria-labelledby="draw-explainer-heading">
    <h2 id="draw-explainer-heading">How Draw picks are built</h2>
    <p>
        Draw publishing targets low-separation fixtures: matches where the model sees enough
        probability that neither side wins in 90 minutes. Draws are structurally harder than many
        side markets — true draw rates in many leagues sit well below home-win rates — so the
        publish threshold and calibration matter even more. A thin Draw card is common and usually
        healthy.
    </p>

    <h3>What the Draw card contains</h3>
    <p>
        Only fixtures whose calibrated draw probability clears the live Draw threshold appear here.
        Each row includes league, teams, kickoff, confidence, and status filters identical in spirit
        to the other market pages, but the underlying decision is different: we are not backing a
        side; we are backing a stalemate. Settlement is simple — full-time scoreline must be level.
    </p>

    <h3>Why draws need their own gate</h3>
    <p>
        Inside 1X2, draw probability comes from the same ensemble as home and away: Shin-devigged
        bookmaker prices, Dixon-Coles score matrix mass on level scorelines, Elo context, and the
        third-party feed when available. After renormalisation, Draw has its own threshold-tuning
        loop so the card does not fill with noise whenever two mid-table sides meet. Optional ML
        classifiers for Draw promote only when they improve holdout Brier versus the incumbent.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Low separation does not automatically mean “publish a draw.”</li>
        <li>A 1–1 or 0–0 lands; a 2–1 late winner does not.</li>
        <li>Historical draw hit rate should be read with sample size in mind.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Reading Draw confidence carefully</h3>
    <p>
        Because base rates are lower, published draw confidences often sit in a tighter band than
        big-side Straight Wins. A 55–65% draw angle can still be a high bar relative to the league’s
        natural draw rate — and it can still lose more often than a casual reader expects. Compare
        today’s published draws against the settled record on <a href="/results">Results</a>, and
        read how promotion gates work on <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>When you see few draws</h3>
    <p>
        Open, high-tempo rounds and clear mismatch fixtures produce few qualifying draws. That is
        preferred over listing every 2.80 decimal draw price on the board. Some selection-list
        variants (including AI draw constructions) appear on <a href="/betslips">Betslips</a> when
        those generators run — they are still subject to the same honesty about uncertainty.
    </p>

    <h3>Responsible use</h3>
    <p>
        Draws are easy to over-bet after a short winning streak. MatchPredictor is an informational
        statistics site for adults (18+), not a bookmaker and not a guarantee service. See
        <a href="/Privacy#responsible-use">responsible use</a> and the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>.
    </p>
</section>
